#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5444" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is invertible? \n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\n- $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/40/407378634431fde7e66d07a36239276173d6ae121f22ef4567cb1f2b28688624.png) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" :question/math-academy-id "e-9985" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ then $\\textrm{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/36/360d6855ad322ec1ffbfdae960754f04522a33ded3e3a7e15711f0099b312ef2.png) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9986" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/e6/e6be87200a2cbb3709d3f398fefad0c547071edb703f88d8859d6ee0c74cc5dd.png) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/32/32e958f4890d733ebc1856a8ca317ff6b52ed797f04a01369d6356c4130240c8.png) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e440973-55fe-51fa-9da0-f4d52cf2e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269685} {:answer/id #uuid "6564e814-315d-5d94-906f-1b6ff52f2ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270172} {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270825} {:answer/id #uuid "cc38f4d0-c9ec-5bea-9900-3e87852c1726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271062} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8a718e-676e-5690-ab51-91d91cda239d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1a5bdd-ecdf-550d-aa85-b91ee12261f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270825} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab21a93-10c6-5a81-9fe7-a6f973ee6db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cac76f2-abeb-58d4-87e9-5f3619bedd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140085" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079f5dae-bfcf-5b50-ba4b-ae83bc6f55b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1d9335-bfdb-5b54-9538-0501d53fbad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Null}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270657} {:answer/id #uuid "abe72a0c-363a-5a3a-8cac-526a5e9222e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Ker}(T)$ and $\\text{Domain}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270803} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ee4548-b055-5785-a92c-1b09684993d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Domain}(T)$ and $\\text{Range}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270889} {:answer/id #uuid "c93ea908-b041-5ad1-8668-bc8878031acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{det}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079f5dae-bfcf-5b50-ba4b-ae83bc6f55b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269339} :answer-field/id #uuid "222b73f0-3a82-5bff-b427-7650cd47a873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55000931-5b53-577e-bfdc-cc3083a1d40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141454" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & ={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom the missing expression are $\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af878bc-a2d4-5d29-9f1c-9e4a3cf3f993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Domain}(T)$ and $n-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269487} {:answer/id #uuid "b89bcde2-5886-5353-b2fb-ee39f615fc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $\\text{det}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270900} {:answer/id #uuid "baa5abde-92f5-5f9a-af2b-a0a103048cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270915} {:answer/id #uuid "debd7b9d-554a-50be-977a-608d85a334ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R∖{0}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271222} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd4fc3-738a-5af8-a034-a398ae8afd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd4fc3-738a-5af8-a034-a398ae8afd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271274} :answer-field/id #uuid "2be4f7f0-fda8-5056-81c8-7f9c5bad1fba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0c34404-67b0-5bd2-86a3-b5bd22c70fb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141457" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6e6c5d7-e923-5b79-aeca-92ce53c73074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449640} {:answer/id #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709274c3-c137-53e6-a5e6-794fdfe9a85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449641} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d40a1ee-aefc-57fa-8071-7deb8e4a3305" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7181c58-f5c1-540e-9c79-9a178187acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449642} {:answer/id #uuid "451e520e-366e-5d15-a3ef-e0067131fc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7181c58-f5c1-540e-9c79-9a178187acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449642} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ef3a30-34f3-5c51-826c-d16c38c6f3d2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8801d71e-6668-5c1b-93c6-53060eac64bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449644} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff3c94-ccfa-5b45-b2dc-3dd9301a301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff3c94-ccfa-5b45-b2dc-3dd9301a301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449645} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1e1146-8542-54b0-b796-f3070052fa06" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is not invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429117a3-6e54-516f-90dc-8b3c46852d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269870} {:answer/id #uuid "540ff262-b6d8-55d6-bdff-7a8f7374caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270017} {:answer/id #uuid "953e5319-0e76-5d91-be9e-97acbe17adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270577} {:answer/id #uuid "c637029f-ce11-54ac-92f8-b41a82831cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271015} {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f27f32f7-f9d9-50bf-b772-90b1d8509143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271401} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f70c062-2c20-514b-a050-6ed7cd19ecf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141495" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a88473c7-29eb-5d23-9326-18bd3c99fbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123309} {:answer/id #uuid "e7983005-874e-5891-b110-3d0ef62d5bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123310} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf948e-0f5a-5d04-a7d0-6da29ac08a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123311} {:answer/id #uuid "686cd5ae-f5fb-5c23-a23d-08b277ca72a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123312} {:answer/id #uuid "69d8cedf-2717-58d0-9115